steps.body:
A constructor runs when an object is created. It has the same name as the class and no return type. Set the members in a **member initializer list** after a colon, so each one is built directly with its value instead of being assigned later in the body.

```cpp
class Student {
public:
    Student(const std::string& name, int id) : name_(name), id_(id) {}
    void print() const { std::cout << name_ << " #" << id_ << '\n'; }
private:
    std::string name_;
    int id_;
};

Student ada("Ada", 1042);   // calls the constructor
Student bob{"Bob", 7};      // braces call it too
ada.print();
bob.print();
```

- **Overloads:** write several constructors with different parameters, just like overloaded functions.
- **Default constructor:** one with no parameters, used by `Student s;`. Once you write any constructor, the compiler stops generating it for you. Write `Student s;`, not `Student s();`, which declares a function.
- **explicit:** mark single-argument constructors `explicit`. Without it, `Student s = name;` or passing a `std::string` where a `Student` is expected silently builds a `Student`.

Members are initialized in the order they are declared in the class, not the order of the list, so keep both orders the same. A `const` member can only be set in the list. A member left out of the list keeps its default member initializer. Unlike the aggregate structs of the last lesson, a class with a constructor works with `emplace_back`: `students.emplace_back("Ada", 1042);` builds the object in place.

example.code:
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

class Student {
public:
    Student() : name_("Unknown"), id_(0) {}
    explicit Student(const std::string& name) : name_(name), id_(0) {}
    Student(const std::string& name, int id) : name_(name), id_(id) {}

    void print() const {
        std::cout << name_ << " #" << id_ << '\n';
    }

private:
    std::string name_;
    int id_;
};

int main() {
    const Student ada("Ada", 1042);
    const Student grace("Grace");
    const Student blank;
    ada.print();
    grace.print();
    blank.print();
    return 0;
}

