#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

class Product {
public:
    Product(const std::string& name, double price, int quantity)
        : name_(name), price_(price), quantity_(quantity) {}
    Product(const std::string& name, double price) : name_(name), price_(price), quantity_(1) {}
    Product() : name_("Unnamed"), price_(0.0), quantity_(0) {}

    void print() const {
        std::cout << name_ << " x" << quantity_ << ": " << price_ * quantity_ << '\n';
    }

private:
    std::string name_;
    double price_;
    int quantity_;
};

int main() {
    std::cout << std::fixed << std::setprecision(2);
    const Product notebook("Notebook", 3.25, 4);
    const Product backpack("Backpack", 24.99);
    const Product blank;
    notebook.print();
    backpack.print();
    blank.print();
    return 0;
}

steps.body:
A destructor runs when an object dies. It is named `~ClassName()`, takes no parameters, and has no return type. You never call it yourself: C++ calls it automatically when the object's lifetime ends.

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

class Tracer {
public:
    explicit Tracer(const std::string& name) : name_(name) {
        std::cout << "open " << name_ << '\n';
    }

    {{code}}

private:
    std::string name_;
};

int main() {
    Tracer a("a");
    {
        Tracer b("b");
        std::cout << "inner block ends\n";
    }
    std::cout << "main ends\n";
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
~Tracer() {
    std::cout << "close " << name_ << '\n';
}
```

When does a local object die?

- At the closing brace of the block that declared it, so an object in an inner `{ }` block dies there.
- When its function returns, because the function's stack frame goes away.
- In **reverse order of construction**: the last object created is the first destroyed. Later objects may depend on earlier ones, so they are torn down first.

This order is guaranteed, which makes destructor output fully predictable. Watch out for an object with no name: `Tracer("x");` creates a temporary that dies at the end of that same statement. Objects in `main` die after `return 0;`, just before the program exits.

example.code:
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

class Tracer {
public:
    explicit Tracer(const std::string& name) : name_(name) {
        std::cout << "open " << name_ << '\n';
    }

    ~Tracer() {
        std::cout << "close " << name_ << '\n';
    }

private:
    std::string name_;
};

int main() {
    Tracer a("a");
    Tracer b("b");
    {
        Tracer c("c");
        std::cout << "inner block ends\n";
    }
    std::cout << "main ends\n";
    return 0;
}

