steps.body:
**RAII** (Resource Acquisition Is Initialization) ties a resource to an object: acquire it in the constructor, release it in the destructor. Because destructors run automatically, cleanup happens on every exit path, including an early `return`. Say `IntBuffer` is a class that allocates an int array in its constructor and frees it in its destructor:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

class IntBuffer {
public:
    explicit IntBuffer(int size) : size_(size), data_(new int[size]()) {
        std::cout << "allocated " << size_ << " ints\n";
    }

    ~IntBuffer() {
        delete[] data_;
        std::cout << "freed " << size_ << " ints\n";
    }

    IntBuffer(const IntBuffer&) = delete;
    IntBuffer& operator=(const IntBuffer&) = delete;

private:
    int size_;
    int* data_;
};

{{code}}

int main() {
    process(true);
    process(false);
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
void process(bool skip) {
    IntBuffer buffer(100);
    if (skip) {
        return; // buffer is freed here
    }
    // ... use buffer
}              // and here
```

A class that owns a raw pointer must not be copied naively. The default copy copies the pointer, not the array, so both objects would `delete[]` the same block: a double delete. Forbid copying with two lines:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

class IntBuffer {
public:
    explicit IntBuffer(int size) : size_(size), data_(new int[size]()) {}
    ~IntBuffer() {
        delete[] data_;
    }

    {{code}}

private:
    int size_;
    int* data_;
};

int main() {
    IntBuffer a(5);
    std::cout << std::boolalpha;
    std::cout << "copyable: " << std::is_copy_constructible<IntBuffer>::value << '\n';
    std::cout << "assignable: " << std::is_copy_assignable<IntBuffer>::value << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
IntBuffer(const IntBuffer&) = delete;
IntBuffer& operator=(const IntBuffer&) = delete;
```

The first stops `IntBuffer b = a;`. The second stops assigning `b = a;` between two existing buffers. A later lesson writes real deep copies.

In everyday code, use `std::vector` and `std::string`: they are RAII classes that own their heap buffers. A class whose members are only such types needs no destructor at all and copies correctly. That is the **rule of zero**.

example.code:
#include <iostream>

class IntBuffer {
public:
    explicit IntBuffer(int size) : size_(size), data_(new int[size]()) {
        std::cout << "allocated " << size_ << " ints\n";
    }

    ~IntBuffer() {
        delete[] data_;
        std::cout << "freed " << size_ << " ints\n";
    }

    IntBuffer(const IntBuffer&) = delete;
    IntBuffer& operator=(const IntBuffer&) = delete;

    void set(int i, int value) {
        data_[i] = value;
    }

    int size() const {
        return size_;
    }

    int sum() const {
        int total = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < size_; ++i) {
            total += data_[i];
        }
        return total;
    }

private:
    int size_;
    int* data_;
};

int main() {
    {
        IntBuffer buffer(5);
        for (int i = 0; i < buffer.size(); ++i) {
            buffer.set(i, (i + 1) * 10);
        }
        std::cout << "sum " << buffer.sum() << '\n';
        // IntBuffer copy = buffer; would not compile: copying is deleted
    }
    std::cout << "after block\n";
    return 0;
}

